    'eyes-barleychain109-1020': { pri: 1, group: G2, title: 'Barley in mail (ART-109), his flail and a battleaxe drawn by code in his hands, every row (10-20)', pt: 'the bestiary §34 (tools/barley-sheet.py chain; deep16/art/barley_p3; deep16/js/sprites.js S.wield; js/save.js SV.look mail) · eyes',
      url: 'deep16/?barleyshow&look=chain&fresh=1',
      look: 'Your 10-20 ("barley barefoot until he\'s wearing chain shirt+ AC gear", "try code flail and battle axe implementation", "that new barley sheet is to use the best of the broken, as if it was good enough"). Beat 1 is the rule: no armour, leather, hide stay the coat; a chain shirt, chain mail, splint put him in the chainmail and boots. Then the chain look through every row with the threshing flail drawn by the engine (the staff through both fists, the beater swinging on its link and out straight on the blow), the same two blows with a battleaxe, Second Wind, Action Surge, the Dodge and a potion and a hand free (one fist lets go, the other keeps the weapon), down with the flail on the floor, flat and up. 15 of its 70 slots are attempts the desk marked broken (the NE views shallow, E half-turned, the N/NE ground rolls, N Interact\'s extra arm). What to look for: whether the code weapon reads as held at game size, frames where it sits wrong in the hands (the review page dev/shots/barley-chain/review.html on dragonsleep-barleychain shows every frame), and the SE NE E walks, which land every step on one leg. ?nowield turns the weapon off.' },
    'eyes-barleycoat77-1020': { pri: 1, group: G2, title: 'Barley in his coat, every view filled: ART-77 and the third pass folded in, SE NE E walks on his own figure (10-20)', pt: 'the bestiary §34 (tools/barley-sheet.py coat; deep16/art/barley_p2) · eyes',
      url: 'deep16/?barleyshow&look=coat&fresh=1',
      look: 'Your 10-20 ("make the best of what we have"): the coat sheet with the second shot (ART-77) and ART-76\'s third pass cut in -- Second Wind and Action Surge standing in all five views (your "him squatting" redrawn), Fall/Prone/GetUp S, GetUp N and SE, Victory N, Overhead N, the N walk\'s grip -- so no view borrows another any more. His walk is his own figure in all eight facings now: the SE NE E attempts each land every step on the same leg (the desk could not draw them otherwise; the old LPC walk was the alternative, another man). What to look for: the S facing\'s fall and get-up, the standing bonus actions, and whether the one-leg hitch at SE NE E reads worse than the old walk did (one line puts it back).' },
